useEffect hook is being used without a dependency array. This causes your application to trigger whatever is inside of useEffect whenever the state of your component changes. This can cause hard to catch bugs and infinite loops.

This is the documentation that explains the reason dependencies are almost all the times necessary to optimize your application
https://reactjs.org/docs/hooks-effect.html#tip-optimizing-performance-by-skipping-effects

Below is an example of how `useEffect` should be used.
```
useEffect(() => {
  // apply any logic you need when the component is mounted

  return = () => {
    // apply any cleanup you need when the component is unmounted
  }
}, [])

useEffect(() => {
  // apply any logic you need when dep1 or dep2 gets updated
}, [dpe1, dep2])

```
Also I noted the `headerScrolling()` function in [this line](https://github.com/alexpadillarosas/gps-on-vermont/pull/4/files#diff-f5da68d8fb8dc6ce1f856de863fd37adbc43e88ae04d5f05ecf14585a4c86ae9L2), is directly manipulating the DOM by adding classes to the body for every page in your application, ideally that would be moved to `BaseLayout` kind of component that you can add or remove the `classeName` directly to the top component in the page without the need to add those classes to the <body> tag.

This PR has also some extra changes that you can ignore, because they were all applied by my IDE on autosaving.

